@page "/filtering"

<CatHead Title="Filtering">
    Narrowing a long list down to what the reader is after: facets in a side panel, the filters that
    apply shown as chips above the results, the reader's own saved views, and conditions combined with
    AND and OR. Every piece is the library's existing form, chip, menu and drawer classes; the app keeps
    the filters, applies them and stores the views. A filter per column belongs on the
    <a href="/table">table</a> itself, and a short bar of them in a <a href="/toolbar">toolbar</a>.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Filtering.SidePanel" Title="Filter side panel" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        A <code>.sedna-split</code> with the panel first, so it sits at the inline start and stacks above the
        results once the list would get too narrow. Each facet is a <code>fieldset.form-section</code> of
        checkboxes, each with its <code>.form-check-count</code> &mdash; the rows the list would show with
        that choice, so a choice that empties the list is visibly a zero and is disabled. The panel's
        <code>.card-foot--split</code> holds Reset and an Apply that says what it will show.
    </p>
    <p>
        Above the results, every filter that applies is a <code>.chip--active</code> whose dismiss button
        removes that one, then Clear all, then the count of what is left. The chips are what the reader
        checks after coming back to the page, so they show each filter in words, not as a number.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Filtering.FilterDrawer" Title="Drawer on a narrow screen" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live.</strong> Where there is no room for the panel, the same facets go in a
        <code>dialog.drawer.drawer--start</code> opened from a Filters button, which shows how many filters
        apply in a <code>.badge</code> and says it in its <code>aria-label</code>. The form is the drawer's
        body, which scrolls on its own, so the footer's Reset and Apply stay in view however long the
        facets get. A Blazor app renders its filter form as one component in both places and picks the
        panel or the drawer by the width it has.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Filtering.SavedViews" Title="Saved views" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live.</strong> The reader's own named filters, switched from a <code>.menu</code> whose
        trigger names the view in use; the current one carries <code>aria-current="true"</code> and a filled
        icon, and the default is noted with <code>.menu-item-note</code>. Once the filters no longer match the
        view, a <code>.badge--warn</code> says Modified beside the trigger, with Save view and Discard changes
        next to it. Save as new view opens the app's own <code>dialog.modal</code> for the name. The app
        stores the views and decides what a modified view is &mdash; with only a few views, a
        <a href="/segmented">segmented control</a> in the same place does the switching.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Filtering.QueryBuilder" Title="Query builder" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        Conditions that combine with AND or OR, in groups nested inside one another.
        <code>.rule-group</code> is the frame: a <code>.rule-group-head</code> with the group's
        <code>.segmented</code> AND/OR choice and its own buttons, then a <code>.rule-group-body</code>
        indented from the head with a line down its inline-start edge, holding the
        <code>.rule-group-row</code> conditions, nested <code>.rule-group</code>s and the
        <code>.rule-group-actions</code>. A row is the field, the operator, the value and the remove button,
        in that order; in a group under 480px wide its fields go one under the other, with the remove button
        beside the first. What the conditions mean, and turning them into a query, is the app's.
    </p>
</CatExample>
